import{_ as t,o,c as r,ae as a}from"./chunks/framework.Dn6zxbF8.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Google Cloud: Artifact Registry","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adrs/6497710059/references/gcp.md","filePath":"adrs/6497710059/references/gcp.md"}'),s={name:"adrs/6497710059/references/gcp.md"};function i(c,e,d,n,l,p){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="google-cloud-artifact-registry" tabindex="-1">Google Cloud: Artifact Registry <a class="header-anchor" href="#google-cloud-artifact-registry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Google Cloud: Artifact Registry&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Verified against primary documentation on 2026-10-16.</p><h2 id="names" tabindex="-1">Names <a class="header-anchor" href="#names" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Names&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An image is addressed as <code>LOCATION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT-ID/REPOSITORY/IMAGE</code>, with <code>:TAG</code> or <code>@IMAGE-DIGEST</code> after it. The location is the first part of the hostname, &quot;the regional or multi-regional location of the repository&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/docker/names" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Repository and image names</a>). <code>IMAGE</code> is &quot;the name of the image in the repository&quot;. The project and the repository are therefore both fixed before our own path starts, which is why the ADR treats <code>&lt;location&gt;-docker.pkg.dev/&lt;project&gt;</code> as the registry prefix.</p><p>Not stated by Google, so not verified:</p><ul><li>The character grammar of a repository name. The create guide says only that &quot;For each repository location in a project, repository names must be unique&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/create-repos" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Create standard repositories</a>), and the REST reference describes <code>repositoryId</code> as &quot;The repository id to use for this repository&quot; with no constraint (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/reference/rest/v1/projects.locations.repositories/create" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">repositories.create</a>). The lowercase <code>[a-z0-9-]</code> rule in the ADR is our own and sits inside any plausible grammar, but Google&#39;s grammar is not documented in the pages checked.</li><li>Length limits for repository, image or tag names. No page checked states one, and the quotas page was not reachable.</li><li>Whether a standard repository accepts an image name with <code>/</code> in it. The only nested example is a remote repository that proxies GitHub Container Registry, pulled as <code>us-west1-docker.pkg.dev/my-project/my-repo/owner-name/test-image:staging</code> (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/docker/pushing-and-pulling" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Push and pull images</a>). The ADR&#39;s paths <code>images/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code>, <code>charts/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;name&gt;</code> and <code>wasm-components/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;package&gt;</code> rely on a nested image name in a standard repository, so this stays unverified.</li></ul><h2 id="repositories" tabindex="-1">Repositories <a class="header-anchor" href="#repositories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Repositories&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each repository holds one format: &quot;Each repository can contain artifacts for a single supported format&quot;, and the formats include Docker, Generic, Maven, npm and Python (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/create-repos" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Create standard repositories</a>). The Docker quickstart creates the repository first (&quot;Create a Docker repository to store the sample image&quot;), and the push guide says &quot;If the target repository does not exist, create a new repository&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/docker/pushing-and-pulling" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Push and pull images</a>). The one exception is the legacy <code>gcr.io</code> domain, whose repositories are created on first push (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Repository overview</a>). No page checked says outright that a push to a missing repository on <code>docker.pkg.dev</code> fails, so &quot;must be created first&quot; rests on the guides above.</p><p>Helm charts and other OCI artifacts live in Docker-format repositories. The supported formats page says &quot;Store Docker container images and Helm charts packaged in OCI format&quot; and &quot;You can store artifacts that support the OCI image format in Artifact Registry Docker repositories&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/supported-formats" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Supported formats</a>). The Helm guide repeats it: &quot;Artifact Registry lets you store and manage Helm charts in Docker-format repositories&quot;, pushed with <code>helm push hello-chart-0.1.0.tgz oci://us-west1-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT/quickstart-helm-repo</code>. &quot;The chart name is the image name&quot; and &quot;The chart version is the image tag&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/helm/store-helm-charts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Store Helm charts</a>), which matches the ADR&#39;s description of what Helm appends.</p><p>Not verified: Wasm components. No page checked mentions Wasm. They are OCI artifacts, so the general OCI statement above is the only support, and no Google page promises that a <code>wkg</code> push of a Wasm media type is accepted.</p><p>One repository per kind (<code>images</code>, <code>charts</code>, <code>wasm-components</code>) is therefore allowed but not required: a single Docker-format repository could hold all of them. Separate repositories are our choice, and they are what let cleanup policies and IAM differ per kind.</p><h2 id="remote-repositories" tabindex="-1">Remote repositories <a class="header-anchor" href="#remote-repositories" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Remote repositories&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Remote repositories &quot;act as proxies&quot; and are read-only. On the first request the repository &quot;downloads and caches&quot; the artifact, and &quot;Artifact Registry serves the cached copy&quot; for the same version afterwards (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Repository overview</a>, <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/remote-overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Remote repositories overview</a>).</p><ul><li>One upstream per repository. No page states the count in words. The upstream is a single setting (&quot;Remote repository upstream source&quot;) that &quot;can&#39;t be changed after the repository is created&quot;, the gcloud flag <code>--remote-docker-repo</code> takes one value, <code>docker-hub</code> or one HTTPS URI, and the preset for Docker Hub is <code>DOCKER-HUB</code> at <code>https://registry-1.docker.io</code> (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/remote-repo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Create remote repositories</a>, <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/artifacts/repositories/create" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">gcloud create</a>). A virtual repository is the product that aggregates several upstreams. So <code>mirrors-docker-hub</code> and <code>mirrors-quay</code> as separate remote repositories follows from the design, but it is an inference.</li><li>Tag refresh. Mutable metadata &quot;are updated from the upstream source when they become more than the default age&quot;, and the listed default for the Docker tag list cache is 1 hour (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/remote-overview" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Remote repositories overview</a>). The quickstart says &quot;If you pull the same tagged image again, it will be pulled from your remote repository&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/create-dockerhub-remote-repository" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Docker Hub remote quickstart</a>). No page says when an already cached tag is moved to a new upstream digest, so the ADR&#39;s statement that a cache refresh moves a mirrored tag is not confirmed, and the 1 hour figure covers the tag list only.</li><li>Path. The quickstart pulls <code>us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT/quickstart-docker-hub-remote/busybox:latest</code>, with no <code>library/</code> segment. No page checked mentions <code>library/</code>. This does not confirm the ADR&#39;s <code>mirrors-docker-hub/library/postgres:16</code>, and it suggests the bare <code>mirrors-docker-hub/postgres:16</code> may be what Artifact Registry expects for Docker Hub official images. It needs a live pull to settle.</li></ul><h2 id="tag-immutability" tabindex="-1">Tag immutability <a class="header-anchor" href="#tag-immutability" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tag immutability&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The setting is <strong>Immutable image tags</strong>, for Docker repositories only, and it is off by default. When on, these actions are not permitted: &quot;Delete a tagged image&quot;, &quot;Remove a tag from an image&quot;, &quot;Push an image with a tag that is already used by another version of the image in the repository&quot;, and &quot;Add, remove, or update labels&quot;. The page adds that a tag always points to the same digest, &quot;including the default <code>latest</code> tag&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/update-repo-settings" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Configure repository settings</a>, <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/create-repos" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Create standard repositories</a>). In gcloud it is <code>--immutable-tags</code> (&quot;Tags cannot be deleted or moved to a different image digest, and tagged images cannot be deleted&quot;) and <code>--no-immutable-tags</code> (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/artifacts/repositories/create" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">gcloud create</a>).</p><p>No page checked describes any exemption, allowlist or pattern for tags, which supports the ADR&#39;s claim that none can be exempted. This is absence of documentation, not a stated &quot;cannot&quot;.</p><p>The setting is also a Docker-repository setting, so it is not documented as applying to a remote repository. The ADR already exempts mirrors from the tag rules.</p><p>Consequence for cleanup: &quot;If a repository has immutable tags enabled, tagged artifacts can&#39;t be deleted&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/cleanup-policy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cleanup policies</a>). With immutability on, a cleanup policy cannot expire <code>pr-</code> tags. See the next section.</p><h2 id="cleanup-policies" tabindex="-1">Cleanup policies <a class="header-anchor" href="#cleanup-policies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cleanup policies&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A policy is a <code>Delete</code> or a <code>Keep</code> action plus conditions (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/cleanup-policy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Configure cleanup policies</a>):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tagState</code></td><td><code>tagged</code>, <code>untagged</code> or <code>any</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>tagPrefixes</code></td><td>tag prefixes, needs <code>tagState</code> set to tagged</td></tr><tr><td><code>versionNamePrefixes</code></td><td>version name prefixes</td></tr><tr><td><code>packageNamePrefixes</code></td><td>package name prefixes</td></tr><tr><td><code>olderThan</code></td><td>minimum time since the version was created, as a duration</td></tr><tr><td><code>newerThan</code></td><td>maximum time since the version was created</td></tr><tr><td><code>keepCount</code></td><td>keep the most recent versions, a keep policy only</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Prefixes match the start of a tag: &quot;the prefixes <code>test</code>, and <code>staging</code> would match images with tags <code>testenv</code> and <code>staging-1.5</code>&quot;. &quot;If an artifact matches the criteria in both the delete policy and the keep policy, the artifact is kept&quot;, and a keep policy alone has no effect, so at least one delete policy must exist. The page&#39;s example deletes <code>&quot;tagPrefixes&quot;: [&quot;alpha&quot;, &quot;v0&quot;]</code> with <code>&quot;olderThan&quot;: &quot;30d&quot;</code> and keeps <code>release</code>-prefixed tags. Changes take effect &quot;within approximately one day&quot;, and a dry run is available.</p><p>So expiring <code>pr-</code> and <code>0.0.0-pr-</code> builds is a delete policy with <code>tagState: tagged</code>, the two prefixes and an <code>olderThan</code>. Release tags are never matched by those prefixes, and a keep policy on the version tags is available as a second guard. This composition is an inference from the documented conditions. The page has no <code>pr-</code> example.</p><p>Open points:</p><ul><li>The page says &quot;You can only apply cleanup policies to standard repositories&quot;, yet the remote repository guide lists cleanup policies as an optional setting at creation. The two are not reconciled by the pages checked, so cleanup on <code>mirrors-*</code> repositories is unverified.</li><li>The commit-tagged image of a pull request carries only <code>pr-&lt;commit&gt;</code>, but an image that later gets a release tag is a different tag on the same version. Whether a version is deleted when any one of its tags matches a delete policy and none matches a keep policy is not spelled out.</li><li>Cleanup does not apply to attachments directly (see the next section).</li><li>Because immutable tags block deletion of tagged artifacts, a repository cannot both turn on immutability and expire <code>pr-</code> tags by cleanup policy. This follows from the quoted sentence, but no page shows the two together.</li></ul><h2 id="signatures-and-referrers" tabindex="-1">Signatures and referrers <a class="header-anchor" href="#signatures-and-referrers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Signatures and referrers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Artifact Registry &quot;implements version 1.1 of the Open Container Initiative (OCI) Distribution Specification&quot; (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/reference/docker-api" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Support for the Docker Registry API</a>). That page does not name the referrers endpoint.</p><p>Attachments are the documented mechanism: &quot;Artifact Registry attachments let you store and manage metadata files associated with your artifacts&quot;, and an artifact can have &quot;any number of attachments that refer to it&quot;. They can be listed with Oras and the <code>v1.1-referrers-api</code> distribution spec, for Docker container images, and the page is marked pre-GA. &quot;Cleanup policies don&#39;t apply directly to attachments&quot;, and deleting an artifact deletes the attachments that target its version (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/manage-metadata-with-attachments" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Manage artifact metadata</a>).</p><p>Binary Authorization&#39;s Sigstore check keeps cosign signatures alongside the image in the same Artifact Registry repository, and supports only Artifact Registry repositories (<a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/cv-sigstore-check" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Use the Sigstore signature check</a>). Cosign lists Artifact Registry among the registries it was tested against (<a href="https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/system_config/registry_support/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cosign registry support</a>).</p><p>Not verified:</p><ul><li>That the <code>/v2/&lt;name&gt;/referrers/&lt;digest&gt;</code> endpoint itself is served. The attachments page uses it through Oras, which implies it, but no Google page states it.</li><li>Whether signatures and referrers survive inside a remote repository, or are copied with an image into another registry. The ADR&#39;s rule to copy them with the artifact is a client concern and is not covered by Google pages.</li></ul>',32)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
